---
import codexSkillsImage from "@/assets/my-current-agentic-software-development-workflow/codex-skills-used.png";
import ArticleRail from "@/components/ArticleRail.astro";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const sections = [
  { id: "the-workflow", label: "The workflow" },
  { id: "plan-while-context-is-fresh", label: "Plan while context is fresh" },
  { id: "one-ticket-one-session-one-worktree", label: "One ticket, one session, one worktree" },
  { id: "shortcuts-and-ui-work", label: "Shortcuts and UI work" },
  { id: "parallel-work", label: "Parallel work" },
  { id: "why-i-rarely-read-every-diff", label: "Why I rarely read every diff" },
  { id: "the-tools-can-change", label: "The tools can change" },
  { id: "references", label: "References" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="My current agentic software development workflow"
  description="How I plan, split, implement, test, and merge software with AI coding agents."
>
  <article class="page-stack writing-article">
    <ArticleRail sections={sections} />

    <header class="article-header">
      <a class="back-link" href="/writing">Writing</a>
      <div class="article-heading">
        <p class="article-kicker">Sep 2026</p>
        <h1>My current agentic software development workflow</h1>
        <p class="article-deck">
          Where I spend my attention, what I hand to agents, and why I rarely read every line they
          write
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <section aria-labelledby="the-workflow">
        <h2 id="the-workflow">The workflow</h2>
        <p>
          Between August 6 and September 4, Codex recorded 1,490 skill invocations while I worked.
          The number is not a quality score. It shows that this is my daily workflow, not something
          I tried for a weekend.
        </p>

        <figure class="article-figure analytics-figure">
          <img
            src={codexSkillsImage.src}
            alt="Codex analytics chart showing 1,490 skill invocations between August 6 and September 4, including Implement, Code Review, Grill Me, Browser, Grill With Docs, Shadcn, Research, and Unslop."
            width={codexSkillsImage.width}
            height={codexSkillsImage.height}
            loading="eager"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>
            My Codex skill usage from August 6 to September 4. Volume shows use, not correctness.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="workflow-figure" aria-labelledby="workflow-caption">
          <ol class="workflow-flow">
            <li><span>01</span>Grill</li>
            <li><span>02</span>Spec</li>
            <li><span>03</span>Tickets</li>
            <li><span>04</span>Implement</li>
            <li><span>05</span>Verify</li>
            <li><span>06</span>Merge</li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption id="workflow-caption">
            I stay present while planning. Fresh agent sessions take over once the work is settled.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="plan-while-context-is-fresh">
        <h2 id="plan-while-context-is-fresh">Plan while context is fresh</h2>
        <p>
          I use Matt Pocock's{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills">engineering skills</a>.<sup>
            <a href="#ref-1">1</a>
          </sup>{" "}
          I start a new app with <code>grill-me</code>. Inside a repo, I use{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/grill-with-docs.md">
            <code>grill-with-docs</code>
          </a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This part needs my full attention. The agent asks one question at a time and follows each
          decision until we agree on the behavior and edge cases. Once we agree, I use{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/to-spec.md">
            <code>to-spec</code>
          </a>{" "}
          in the same session. The conversation contains the reasons behind the decisions, so I do
          not throw it away.
        </p>
        <p>
          The spec goes to GitHub Issues. Linear would also work. Then{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/to-tickets.md">
            <code>to-tickets</code>
          </a>{" "}
          splits it into small vertical slices with acceptance criteria and blocking links.
          Bounded tickets keep implementation sessions away from what Dex Horthy calls the{" "}
          <a href="https://ai.engineer/talks/context-engineering-for-complex-codebases">
            "dumb zone"
          </a>, where accumulated context starts hurting the agent's work.<sup>
            <a href="#ref-2">2</a>
          </sup>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="one-ticket-one-session-one-worktree">
        <h2 id="one-ticket-one-session-one-worktree">One ticket, one session, one worktree</h2>
        <p>
          My implementation unit is strict. One ticket gets one session, one Git worktree, and one
          implementation. I point the session at the ticket and invoke{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/implement.md">
            <code>implement</code>
          </a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The agent uses TDD at the agreed test seams, runs type checks as it works, runs the full
          test suite at the end, and calls a separate code-review skill before committing. I add
          Playwright coverage when the ticket includes browser behavior.
        </p>
        <p>
          Testing a ticket to 100 percent means every acceptance criterion has evidence. It does not
          mean 100 percent code coverage or zero chance of a defect.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="shortcuts-and-ui-work">
        <h2 id="shortcuts-and-ui-work">Shortcuts and UI work</h2>
        <p>
          Small changes can skip the spec and ticket phases. Adding a functional menu option or
          fixing a contained bug can go straight to <code>implement</code>. If the behavior or edge
          cases are unclear, I still grill first.
        </p>
        <p>
          For UI work, I attach a reference image and iterate until the result looks right. My eyes
          judge the appearance. Playwright checks behavior such as clicks, navigation, and state.
          Neither check replaces the other.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="parallel-work">
        <h2 id="parallel-work">Parallel work</h2>
        <p>
          The blocking links from <code>to-tickets</code> show what can run at the same time. Each
          unblocked ticket gets a separate session and worktree. I start the implementations, go
          AFK, and wait for Codex notifications.
        </p>
        <p>
          For conflicts, I use{" "}
          <a
            href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/blob/main/docs/engineering/resolving-merge-conflicts.md"
          >
            <code>resolving-merge-conflicts</code>
          </a>. To publish, the official GitHub plugin's{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/openai/plugins/blob/main/plugins/github/skills/yeet/SKILL.md">
            <code>github:yeet</code>
          </a>{" "}
          skill checks the change, commits it, pushes the branch, and opens a draft PR.<sup>
            <a href="#ref-3">3</a>
          </sup>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="why-i-rarely-read-every-diff">
        <h2 id="why-i-rarely-read-every-diff">Why I rarely read every diff</h2>
        <p>
          This still feels strange to say, but I rarely review generated code line by line. I open
          the app, exercise the feature, and confirm it behaves as intended. Then I approve the PR
          and merge it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I trust the chain rather than one model response. The chain includes my decisions, the
          spec, bounded tickets, test seams, acceptance criteria, the test suite, Playwright, and the
          implementation skill's code review. Most of my scrutiny happens before coding. My final
          check focuses on the product.
        </p>
        <p>
          I still scan authentication, billing, migrations, destructive data operations, and other
          changes whose risks tests cannot prove. That is a fast, targeted read. This is what works
          for me, not a promise that the method removes defects or fits every team.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="the-tools-can-change">
        <h2 id="the-tools-can-change">The tools can change</h2>
        <p>
          I started this workflow with Claude Code and later moved to Codex. It is agent-agnostic.
          The agent needs to follow skill files, use isolated worktrees, run project tools, and
          report which checks passed.
        </p>
        <p>
          I use Cursor's{" "}
          <a href="https://www.skills.sh/cursor/plugins/unslop"><code>unslop</code> skill</a> for
          product copy, documentation, PR text, and agent explanations that are too dense to
          understand.<sup><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>
        </p>
        <p>
          Addy Osmani has a similar{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills">agent-skills collection</a> that can
          select skills automatically based on the task.<sup><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup> Matt
          Pocock's main engineering handoffs are invoked by the human. I have not tried Addy's
          collection yet, but I want to.
        </p>
        <p>
          This workflow will change. I want to keep spending my attention while the product is still
          a set of decisions. Once those decisions are explicit, each agent gets one ticket and a
          fresh context window.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-references" aria-labelledby="references">
        <h2 id="references">References</h2>
        <ol>
          <li id="ref-1">
            Matt Pocock,{" "}
            <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills">Engineering skills</a>.
          </li>
          <li id="ref-2">
            Dex Horthy,{" "}
            <a href="https://ai.engineer/talks/context-engineering-for-complex-codebases">
              No Vibes Allowed: Solving Hard Problems in Complex Codebases
            </a>.
          </li>
          <li id="ref-3">
            OpenAI,{" "}
            <a href="https://github.com/openai/plugins/blob/main/plugins/github/skills/yeet/SKILL.md">
              GitHub Yeet skill
            </a>.
          </li>
          <li id="ref-4">
            Cursor,{" "}
            <a href="https://www.skills.sh/cursor/plugins/unslop">Unslop editing skill</a>.
          </li>
          <li id="ref-5">
            Addy Osmani,{" "}
            <a href="https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills">Agent Skills</a>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
